From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 20:35:52 +0000
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: describe what the CPU-launch firmware owns

The secondary VPEs on this SoC are not started by Linux. AVM's resident
stage-1 loader has already woken them by the time the kernel gains
control and parks them in a wait loop, with EVA segmentation configured,
waiting for a launch command in a mailbox at the very bottom of RAM. All
three of the facts needed to use that mechanism were missing from the
device tree.

The first is the memory. Nothing below 0x20400000 belongs to Linux on an
EVA-booted board: the mailbox sits at 0x20000000, the loader's reboot
string at 0x20001000, the loader itself at 0x20010000, and four further
megabytes of loader scratch at 0x20100000. Declaring none of them was
survivable only for as long as nobody looked, because MIPS runs memblock
bottom-up during early boot and the first early allocation -- the
unflattened copy of the device tree, 13158 bytes -- lands squarely on the
mailbox, with the allocations behind it walking into the loader's text.
Read under our kernel, every CPU slot in that mailbox therefore contains
device tree, and every CPU looks dead. The reservations are what make the
mailbox readable at all; the rest of the series is downstream of them.

The 4 MiB block at 0x20100000 is reserved because AVM reserves it, not
because its contents are known. The stacks the parked VPEs are sitting on
are a plausible tenant, which would make handing that range to the buddy
allocator survivable right up until SMP starts working. Narrowing it is
future work and needs hardware, not reasoning.

The second is the doorbell. Each parked CPU is released by ringing one
specific GIC shared interrupt, and which one is firmware truth rather
than an allocation Linux is free to make -- 20, 21 and 85, the same three
values the vendor's own device tree carries. They go on the cpu nodes as
vmb-fw-ipi, and the property is documented in the MIPS cpus binding,
which has unevaluatedProperties: false and would otherwise reject it.

The third is that Linux must leave those three lines alone. The GIC
driver's probe rewrites polarity, trigger and mask across every shared
interrupt it can see, which would undo the firmware's configuration of
exactly the lines the launch depends on. mti,reserved-list names them;
the driver side of that property follows in the next commit.

The reservations live in seale_avm.dtsi rather than xrx500.dtsi because
a reservation cannot be stated without the memory node it comes out of,
and easy350_anywan does not boot this way. The doorbells are SoC truth
and go in xrx500.dtsi.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mips/cpus.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mips/cpus.yaml
index d3677f53f142..c151e66929f9 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mips/cpus.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mips/cpus.yaml
@@ -50,6 +50,17 @@ properties:
 
   device_type: true
 
+  vmb-fw-ipi:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
+    description:
+      GIC shared interrupt number of the doorbell that releases this CPU from
+      the wait loop a resident firmware parked it in. Used on systems whose
+      secondary VPEs are launched through a firmware mailbox instead of by the
+      kernel itself, such as the Intel/Lantiq xRX500 (interAptiv). The value is
+      fixed by that firmware, and the same interrupts are normally listed in
+      the GIC's mti,reserved-list so that Linux leaves them configured as the
+      firmware left them.
+
 allOf:
   - $ref: /schemas/opp/opp-v1.yaml#
   - if:
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
index 2900f83e4de4..fb7cf5cd43f7 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
@@ -41,6 +41,40 @@ reserved-memory {
 		#address-cells = <1>;
 		#size-cells = <1>;
 		ranges;
+
+		/*
+		 * The bottom of RAM belongs to the resident boot loader, not
+		 * to Linux. MIPS runs memblock bottom-up and
+		 * early_init_fdt_scan_reserved_mem() runs before
+		 * bootmem_init(), so without these no-map reservations the
+		 * first early allocations land on the firmware mailbox and on
+		 * the loader's own image.
+		 */
+		vmb_mailbox: vmb@20000000 {
+			reg = <0x20000000 0x1000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		avm_reboot_string: avm-reboot-string@20001000 {
+			reg = <0x20001000 0x200>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		urlader: urlader@20010000 {
+			reg = <0x20010000 0x30000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Four megabytes immediately below the kernel load address;
+		 * the vendor reserves the same range for its own crash log. A
+		 * board that places ramoops inside this range must narrow
+		 * this node to match.
+		 */
+		avm_reserved: avm-reserved@20100000 {
+			reg = <0x20100000 0x400000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
 	};
 };
 
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
index 55b31c68c630..8600e26d1548 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
@@ -90,6 +90,17 @@ ramoops@20100000 {
 			mem-type = <1>;
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * The rest of the region seale_avm.dtsi reserves, whose first
+		 * megabyte the crash log above has taken.
+		 */
+		/delete-node/ avm-reserved@20100000;
+
+		avm-reserved@20200000 {
+			reg = <0x20200000 0x300000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
 		/*
 		 * Owned by the second CPU's boot loader (the vendor ships its
 		 * own device tree for that core); no-map so this kernel never
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
index e32f4a73f4c3..fc41a0668e7b 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
@@ -31,18 +31,21 @@ cpu1: cpu@1 {
 			device_type = "cpu";
 			compatible = "mti,interaptiv";
 			reg = <1>;
+			vmb-fw-ipi = <20>;
 		};
 
 		cpu2: cpu@2 {
 			device_type = "cpu";
 			compatible = "mti,interaptiv";
 			reg = <2>;
+			vmb-fw-ipi = <21>;
 		};
 
 		cpu3: cpu@3 {
 			device_type = "cpu";
 			compatible = "mti,interaptiv";
 			reg = <3>;
+			vmb-fw-ipi = <85>;
 		};
 	};
 
@@ -68,6 +71,12 @@ gic: gic@12320000 {
 		 */
 		interrupt-parent = <&cpu_intc>;
 		mti,reserved-ipi-vectors = <56 8>;
+
+		/*
+		 * The doorbells the CPU-launch firmware owns. They are shared
+		 * GIC interrupts and must stay out of the kernel's IPI pool.
+		 */
+		mti,reserved-list = <20 21 85>;
 	};
 
 	cgu: cgu@16200000 {
